## 5. Release Notes and Changelog Automation

Both Google Play and TestFlight allow distributing release notes ("What's New") to testers so QA engineers understand which features, fixes, and ticket IDs are included in each build.

### Android What's New Directory Structure
The `google-play-deploy@3` step automatically detects language-specific release notes organized under an `android/whatsnew/` directory:

```text
android/
├── app/
│   ├── build.gradle
│   └── src/
└── whatsnew/
    ├── whatsnew-en-US
    ├── whatsnew-es-ES
    └── whatsnew-de-DE
```

Each file contains plain text describing the release (maximum 500 characters per file):
```text
Release Build 248 (Sprint 42):
- Feature: Added biometrics authentication support for Android 14.
- Fix: Resolved memory leak during offline database synchronization.
- Security: Patched deep-link intent vulnerabilities identified by NowSecure.
```

### Dynamic Git Changelog Script
Rather than manually editing text files, add a brief **Script** step in Bitrise prior to the deployment step to auto-generate the changelog from Git commit messages since the last tag:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

echo "=== Generating Release Changelog from Git Commits ==="
mkdir -p android/whatsnew

PREVIOUS_TAG=$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 HEAD^ 2>/dev/null || echo "")

if [ -z "$PREVIOUS_TAG" ]; then
  CHANGELOG="Initial automated Bitrise release build #${BITRISE_BUILD_NUMBER}."
else
  CHANGELOG=$(git log "${PREVIOUS_TAG}..HEAD" --oneline --no-merges | head -n 5)
fi

echo "$CHANGELOG" > android/whatsnew/whatsnew-en-US
echo "Generated release notes for build ${BITRISE_BUILD_NUMBER}:"
cat android/whatsnew/whatsnew-en-US
```

