## 2. The One-Write Rule and Safe Rollback Protocol

Unlike web deployments where servers can be instantly reverted to a previous container hash, mobile stores enforce a strict cryptographic constraint:

<div class="portal-alert-box danger">
  <div class="portal-alert-title">THE ONE-WRITE RULE</div>
  <p>
    <strong>Version codes and bundle versions are strictly monotonic and irreversible.</strong> Once a version code (e.g. <code>versionCode: 248</code>) has been submitted to Google Play or Apple App Store Connect, no binary with that version number or lower can ever be uploaded again, even if the release was deleted, rejected, or revoked.
  </p>
</div>

### Why Rollbacks Do Not Exist in Mobile
When a catastrophic bug reaches production in mobile apps, you **cannot** simply "rollback" the server:
1. Physical devices cache the higher version number. The operating system package manager (Android Package Installer or iOS SpringBoard) refuses to install a binary with a lower version number over an existing higher version.
2. Store APIs reject re-uploading an older binary without an incremented build number.

### The Safe Roll-Forward Protocol
When an artifact fails at Stage 6 or Stage 7:
1. **Never attempt to overwrite the existing tag or build number.**
2. Revert the faulty commit on the release branch using `git revert <commit_hash>`.
3. Allow Bitrise to execute a new release build with an incremented `$BITRISE_BUILD_NUMBER` (e.g. build 249).
4. Bitrise compiles, signs, and distributes the clean build to the internal testing track, instantly superseding the defective build.

```bash
# Emergency Roll-Forward Procedure
git checkout release/v2.4.0
git pull origin release/v2.4.0

# Create a clean revert commit
git revert 4f8a12b -m 1 --no-edit

# Push to trigger automated Bitrise build #249
git push origin release/v2.4.0
```

